description:
persona-core/src/spiffe_id.rs:95-102:

    pub fn provenance(&self) -> Option<&str> {
        let (prefix, source) = self.path.rsplit_once('/')?;
        if prefix.ends_with("via") || prefix == "via" {

Two problems in one line:

1. `|| prefix == "via"` is dead. `"via".ends_with("via")` is true, so the first clause already covers it. Dead code that reads as if it handles a case the first clause misses.
2. `ends_with("via")` is a substring test, not a path-segment test. `spiffe://tailscale/user/trivia/alice` returns `Some("alice")`, as does any path whose second-to-last segment ends in those three letters (`nvidia` does not, but `via`, `trivia`, `Bolivia` do). The doc says "Returns `Some("source")` if the path ends with `via/{source}`" — that is not what it does.

The function is the only accessor for claim provenance, so a wrong `Some` here means a caller attributes an identity to the wrong source. It is currently unused outside persona-core, which is why nothing has caught it.

Fix: `if prefix == "via" || prefix.ends_with("/via")` — that is the correct segment test and the second clause stops being dead.
